\section{Cross a Protocol, Not Just a Wire}

Two domains are asynchronous when no reliable edge relationship can be used to time a transfer. Equal nominal frequencies do not guarantee such a relationship. A receiver sampling a transition near its active edge can go metastable. A two-stage synchronizer for a single-bit level gives the first register additional time to resolve before the second samples it. It reduces the probability of propagating metastability; it does not reduce it to zero or guarantee delivery of every input transition. Use device characterization and implementation guidance, such as the synchronizer and CDC material in the \href{https://docs.amd.com/r/en-US/ug949-vivado-design-methodology}{AMD UltraFast Design Methodology Guide}, rather than estimating reliability from how many registers the RTL happens to show.

At 100 MHz the receiver samples every \(10\) ns. If its edges occur at \(0,10,20,\ldots\) ns, a pulse high from \(2\) to \(6\) ns is never sampled even by an ideal flip-flop. Adding a third stage cannot recover it. For an event use a protocol that keeps an indication pending until it has been observed. For a bus, sampling each bit with a separate chain is not a coherent-word protocol: when \texttt{0111} changes to \texttt{1000}, unrelated bit delays can expose a word that was never sent.

