\section{Synchronize a Held Level, Then Acknowledge Its Use}

The following SystemVerilog \cite{ieee1800systemverilog} fragment synchronizes just the request toggle into the receive domain. The \texttt{ASYNC\_REG} attribute is an AMD synthesis and placement hint; use the appropriate equivalent for other tools. The rest of the protocol does not follow from the attribute.

\begin{verbatim}
(* ASYNC_REG = "TRUE" *) logic req_meta, req_sync;
always_ff @(posedge dst_clk) begin
    if (dst_rst) begin
        req_meta <= 1'b0;
        req_sync <= 1'b0;
    end else begin
        req_meta <= req_src;
        req_sync <= req_meta;
    end
end
\end{verbatim}

For a single outstanding word, the source holds a data register and uses a return acknowledgement toggle through its own synchronizer. Pseudocode below assumes both sides start with zero toggle state and no pending word; neither side resets independently during traffic:

\begin{verbatim}
on source clock:
    if have_word and req == synced_ack:
        held_payload = word           # stay stable until acknowledgement
        req = NOT req                 # at most one outstanding word
on destination clock:
    if synced_req != ack and receiver_can_accept:
        consume(held_payload)         # check bundled-data timing
        ack = synced_req              # allow the source to reuse storage
\end{verbatim}

The invariant is that the source cannot toggle twice without an acknowledgement. A differing synchronized request marks an unconsumed word at the destination; acceptance copies the received toggle into \texttt{ack}, so a stable request cannot deliver it twice. The invariant relies on the toggle crossing eventually resolving and on the payload meeting setup and hold at capture. The destination must capture the payload in a destination-clocked register before it acknowledges it; feeding \texttt{held\_payload} directly into unrelated destination logic would leave that logic exposed to an asynchronous bus. Check the relative timing from the source data register to that destination register against the earliest protocol capture edge. Waiting for the return acknowledgement keeps the source from overwriting the bus early; it does not constrain the outbound routing delay by itself.

\Cref{fig:visual-321} distinguishes the two synchronizer chains from the stable payload connection they govern.

