\section{Constrain the Crossing and Verify Its Assumptions}

Venkataramani and colleagues' C-to-asynchronous-dataflow flow also bundles a data bus with request and acknowledgement, using a four-phase protocol and a matched delay. A clockless stage with those timing obligations is not the same circuit as the two-clock crossing above. In both cases a logical protocol proof needs a separate argument for arrival of the data relative to control.

For sustained traffic, an asynchronous FIFO can decouple rates. Its memory holds the data, while synchronized Gray-coded pointers convey progress; adjacent source pointer values differ in one bit. The physical flow still needs the appropriate maximum delay or skew limits so transitions from successive pointer updates do not overlap arbitrarily at the receiving synchronizer. Synchronize reset release in each domain and specify what happens to pending data across reset. Review CDC paths structurally, verify exactly-once or ordered delivery under variable sampling delays, and check the intra-domain timing and relevant cross-boundary physical bounds separately. A false-path declaration can remove a meaningless periodic timing check, but it cannot enforce the protocol. Logic optimizers and ideal HDL simulation do not model analog resolution; inspect the mapped and placed synchronizer, preserving its stages and keeping unrelated logic out of the metastability-resolution path.
